<% content_for :title, "Authorities" %>
<%#
  THE AUTHORITY OVERVIEW + THE EVICTION CONSOLE.

  Every number on this page is READ OFF THE CHAIN. That is not a style
  preference — the constants disagreed with each other and with the chain when
  this page was built (config.rb said the upgrade Squad was "FOUR at threshold
  3", the hub tile said "2-of-3", docs/SOLANA.md said five at three; the chain
  says THREE OF FIVE on both clusters, with different membership per cluster).
  A page an operator opens mid-incident cannot inherit that.

  NEVER WRITE "MULTISIG" UNQUALIFIED HERE. Say "vault signer set" or "Squads
  upgrade multisig". The unqualified word is what produced the claims above.
%>

<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-8">
  <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold text-heading">Authorities</h1>
    <span class="font-mono text-xs text-muted"><%= @network %></span>
  </div>
  <p class="text-secondary text-sm mb-6">
    Who can do what to this platform, decoded from the chain rather than from
    config. The eviction console leads because it is the only thing this page can
    change; the three authorities are listed under it, and two of them are
    read-only here.
  </p>

  <%# ── THE MAIN EVENT, FIRST ────────────────────────────────────────────────
      The panels below are REFERENCE; this is the act. An operator reaches this
      page mid-incident to remove a key, and burying that control under four
      read-only panels made him scroll past everything he already knew to reach
      the one thing he came for. Ordering is not decoration on a page like this:
      it is what the page says its job is.

      The panels still follow rather than move elsewhere, because the console
      cites them — the slot count, the required signatures and the eligible
      wallets all come from the reads rendered underneath. %>
  <%= render "eviction", rotation: @rotation, plan: @rotation_plan, roster: @roster,
                         vault: @vault, eligible: @eligible_signers,
                         required: @required_signatures, max_slots: @max_slots,
                         server_address: @server_address, identities: @identities %>

  <%= render "authority_vault", vault: @vault, vault_error: @vault_error,
                                server_address: @server_address,
                                max_slots: @max_slots, identities: @identities %>

  <%= render "authority_thresholds", governance: @governance,
                                     governance_pda: @governance_pda,
                                     config_governance: @config_governance,
                                     table: @threshold_table,
                                     network: @network,
                                     readable: @governance_readable,
                                     known: @chain_governance_known %>

  <%= render "authority_squads", squads: @squads, squads_address: @squads_address,
                                 squads_app_url: @squads_app_url,
                                 configured_upgrade_authority: @configured_upgrade_authority,
                                 network: @network, vault: @vault,
                                 identities: @identities %>

  <%# (3) THE SERVER'S OWN SIGNING IDENTITY — named so it stops being mistaken
      for the vault signer set. It is the third "admin" in this codebase and the
      one that has no on-chain meaning of its own. %>
  <section class="card p-4 mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-heading mb-1">Server signing identity</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary text-sm mb-3">
      The key <em>this Rails process</em> signs with. It is only an authority at
      all because it also sits in the vault signer set above — being the server
      confers nothing on chain.
    </p>
    <% if @server_address.present? %>
      <%= render "wallet_identity", address: @server_address,
                 user: @identities[@server_address], compact: true %>
      <%# THREE STATES, NOT TWO. `if @vault && include?` collapses "the read
          FAILED" into "this key is NOT a signer" — a definite negative fact,
          followed by an instruction to act on it, while the panel above says the
          account could not be read. The page would contradict itself, and it is
          reachable today: it is the state CI renders on every run.

          This is the same rule the GovernanceConfig panel already follows —
          UNREAD IS NOT ABSENT — and it has to hold everywhere on this page or it
          holds nowhere. An authority page that asserts a membership fact it
          could not check is the exact failure this page exists to end. %>
      <p class="mt-1 text-xs <%= @vault ? "text-muted" : "text-warning-ink" %>">
        <% if @vault.nil? %>
          The vault signer set could not be read, so this page cannot say whether
          this key is in it — and therefore cannot tell you whether the server can
          lead a rotation. Nothing below is safe to act on until that read
          succeeds.
        <% elsif @vault[:active_signers].include?(@server_address) %>
          In the vault signer set — so it can sign vault actions, and so it
          <strong>cannot be evicted by a rotation it leads</strong> (see the
          console at the top of this page).
        <% else %>
          <strong>Not</strong> in the vault signer set, so it cannot sign vault
          actions at all. An eviction must be led by a wallet that is.
        <% end %>
      </p>
    <% else %>
      <p class="text-sm text-warning-ink">
        This process holds no admin key, so it cannot sign or lead anything.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <div class="json-debug mt-8">
    <pre><%= JSON.pretty_generate(
      network: @network,
      program_id: @program_id,
      vault_pda: @vault && @vault[:pda],
      governance_pda: @governance_pda,
      governance_on_chain: @governance_readable ? @governance.present? : "unread",
      governance_configured: @config_governance,
      squads_multisig: @squads_address,
      squads_threshold: @squads && "#{@squads[:threshold]} of #{@squads[:members].length}",
      squads_vault_pda: @squads && @squads[:vault_pda],
      required_signatures_to_rotate: @required_signatures
    ) %></pre>
  </div>
</div>
